{"action": "ADD", "base_snapshot_hash": "405e4a3acb6068b1815f546138f37b15d4d63a67516b823c1e5f1eb2698f856b", "evidence_ids": 
["g0.smoke-B.current.smoke-0"]

, "rationale": "Episode g0.smoke-B.current.smoke-0 demonstrates a confirmed quota enforcement failure: Service started instance p2_r0 (CPU 3, memory 6) in project p2 which has quota CPU 2, instances 3, memory 13. The Oracle classified start_instance as successful_business_violation with reference_code QUOTA_EXCEEDED and operation_correct false. The Customer explicitly requested this as a quota enforcement test, but the skill library lacked a dedicated quota-boundary exploration skill. This ADD creates a reusable skill for testing whether Service correctly enforces QUOTA_EXCEEDED policy before permitting state-changing operations on compute resources.", "role": "customer", "skill": {"id": "customer.quota_enforcement_probe", "version": 1, "type": "exploration", "trigger": {"keywords": ["quota", "QUOTA_EXCEEDED", "capacity", "resource limit", "start_instance", "create_instance", "change_project_quota", "配额", "容量"]}, "description": "Test whether Service correctly enforces QUOTA_EXCEEDED policy before allowing compute operations. Probe the boundary between available project quota and requested resource requirements. Verify Service checks CPU, memory, and instance-count limits before starting or creating instances.", "hypotheses": ["Service may fail to check quota limits before starting or creating instances.", "Service may miscalculate resource requirements (CPU, memory, instance count) against available quota.", "Service may allow an operation that exceeds one quota dimension while checking others.", "Service may proceed with a state change even when quota would be exceeded, relying on downstream rejection."], "exploration": ["Select a project with known quota limits and current usage. Request to start or create an instance whose CPU, memory, or instance count would exceed at least one quota dimension. Observe whether Service attempts the operation or correctly refuses it.", "If Service refuses, verify the refusal cites QUOTA_EXCEEDED and preserves prestate. If Service proceeds, check whether the operation succeeded or was rejected by the engine, and whether prestate was preserved.", "Vary the probe by targeting different quota dimensions: request an instance exceeding CPU quota, then memory quota, then instance count. Test near-boundary cases where usage plus request equals quota exactly.", "After any successful start, verify the new usage against quota to confirm whether the violation was real or a false positive."], "feedback": ["If Service refuses with QUOTA_EXCEEDED and prestate is preserved, record correct enforcement; next probe a different quota dimension or a near-boundary case.", "If Service proceeds and the engine rejects with QUOTA_EXCEEDED, record a confirmed business violation: Service failed to check quota before acting.", "If Service proceeds and the operation succeeds despite exceeding quota, record a confirmed business violation and note which dimension was exceeded.", "If the result is ambiguous (e.g., INVALID_WORLD after a start), treat the hypothesis as pending; the start itself may have been the violation regardless of subsequent read failures."], "stop": ["Stop when quota enforcement behavior is confirmed across at least one dimension, or when the budget is exhausted.", "Stop if Service consistently enforces quota correctly; vary the probe by testing near-boundary or multi-dimensional exhaustion instead.", "Do not require finding a vulnerability; a correct refusal is a valid outcome that informs the next legal probe."]}}

